      "local_names": "",
      "researcher_notes_unit": "AL2026: So far, Jorf Lasfar or Mohammedia don't appear to be off the table, despite Nador FSRU being suspended. However, the plan for the Atlantic terminal might become more flexible or FSRU-focused, and Morocco’s primary Atlantic LNG entry point still remains unclear. https://medias24.com/2026/04/06/port-de-jorf-lasfar-decryptage-du-nouveau-projet-dextension-1654925/\nCapacity for this terminal might be outdated, but generally seems to be in agreement with the 2024-2030 roadmap needs?",
      "researcher_notes_project": "AL2026: Morocco's original LNG plan: \"The programme centres on three ports – Nador West Med, Dakhla Atlantic (Western Sahara in our data), and either Jorf Lasfar or Mohammedia – and forms part of the country’s 2024–2030 roadmap to improve energy security and reduce fossil fuel dependence.\" With Nador West shelved indefinitely, it's good to check whether the rest of the plan is being reconsidered, especially Jorf Lasfar LNG Terminal. \n- Phase one (2025-2027): it includes the tender, construction, and commercial operation of the Nador West Med LNG terminal and the associated pipelines. It also involves an update of a pre-feasibility study for an Atlantic coast LNG regasification terminal.\n- Phase two (post-2030): involves the delivery of the Atlantic coast LNG regasification terminal, the development of an LNG regasification terminal at Dakhla Atlantic port, and the construction of further pipelines.\n- Phase three (long-term): this phase involves connecting to the Mauritanian and Senegalese gas networks through the Gazoduc Afrique Atlantique pipeline and. Also, it involves developing green hydrogen infrastructure. \nFrom: https://www.constructionbriefing.com/news/moroccos-us6bn-gas-strategy-kicks-off-construction-planning-at-three-ports/8062812.article?zephr_sso_ott=svBcBT\nhttps://constructionreviewonline.com/morocco-reveals-plans-for-700-million-nador-west-med-port-lng-terminal-alongside-major-pipeline-network/",
